      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "ABSTRACT Theodor W. Adorno called out \u2018identitarian thinking\u2019\u2014the imposition of totality over the particular\u2014as the root of societal domination. As a remedy, he developed negative dialectics, highlighting the non-identity of subject-object relations. However, the normative turn in Critical Theory, led by figures like J\u00fcrgen Habermas and others, critiqued Adorno for lacking sufficient normative grounding to analyze social conditions and facilitate emancipation. Against this background, I argue that Adorno\u2019s philosophy prioritizes normative motivations over normative foundations. His non-identitarian pedagogy seeks to motivate individuals to resist complicity in societal wrongs [Nicht Mitmachen]. First, I critique victim-centrism of normative foundations, arguing they fail to motivate victimizers to abstain from wrongdoing. Second, I contend that normative motivations are essential for reconstructing society, requiring both (1) empowering the unempowered and (2) self-denial of unjustified entitlements. Finally, I demonstrate how Adorno\u2019s preponderance of the object grounds non-identitarian pedagogy, cultivating critical rationality and resistance.",
